1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,350
One cell.

2
00:00:01,130 --> 00:00:02,030
Two divisions.

3
00:00:02,018 --> 00:00:02,997
Four new cells.

4
00:00:03,397 --> 00:00:07,230
In the lab book, Aarav saw young
pollen grains sitting in little groups

5
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:08,130
of four.

6
00:00:07,889 --> 00:00:09,960
Each group came from just one cell.

7
00:00:10,410 --> 00:00:15,599
He loves number puzzles, and he asked:
meiosis one already halved the chromosomes.

8
00:00:15,599 --> 00:00:18,413
So why does the cell divide a second time?

9
00:00:18,763 --> 00:00:22,695
Because after meiosis one, each
chromosome still has two chromatids.

10
00:00:22,695 --> 00:00:25,840
The second division, meiosis
two, pulls them apart.

11
00:00:26,340 --> 00:00:30,394
By the end, you will walk through
meiosis two, and say why meiosis matters

12
00:00:30,394 --> 00:00:32,991
for every living thing
that reproduces sexually.

13
00:00:33,641 --> 00:00:35,624
First, a quick look at where we are.

14
00:00:35,974 --> 00:00:38,432
After meiosis one, we have two cells.

15
00:00:38,432 --> 00:00:40,795
Each has one set of chromosomes, n.

16
00:00:40,795 --> 00:00:43,918
But each chromosome still
has two sister chromatids.

17
00:00:44,368 --> 00:00:47,468
Between the two divisions comes
interkinesis, a short pause.

18
00:00:47,468 --> 00:00:49,070
The DNA is not copied again.

19
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:51,519
Meiosis two works like mitosis.

20
00:00:51,519 --> 00:00:55,926
It separates the sister chromatids,
and keeps the number the same, so it

21
00:00:55,926 --> 00:00:57,630
is an equational division.

22
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:01,607
Let's zoom in, to the cells
right after meiosis one.

23
00:01:01,957 --> 00:01:06,219
Inside the anther, each pollen
mother cell has finished meiosis one.

24
00:01:06,719 --> 00:01:07,619
Zoom in.

25
00:01:07,447 --> 00:01:09,302
Two cells sit side by side.

26
00:01:09,302 --> 00:01:11,695
This pair is called a dyad of cells.

27
00:01:12,195 --> 00:01:13,793
Zoom into one cell.

28
00:01:13,793 --> 00:01:17,730
In our example it has just
two chromosomes, one set.

29
00:01:18,230 --> 00:01:19,801
Zoom into one chromosome.

30
00:01:19,801 --> 00:01:23,782
Two sister chromatids, joined at one
centromere, with a kinetochore on

31
00:01:23,782 --> 00:01:24,682
each side.

32
00:01:25,217 --> 00:01:28,820
Now let's run meiosis two,
in both cells at once.

33
00:01:29,170 --> 00:01:30,070
Stage one.

34
00:01:30,033 --> 00:01:31,045
Prophase two.

35
00:01:31,045 --> 00:01:35,329
It starts right after the first
division, and it is much simpler.

36
00:01:35,329 --> 00:01:39,941
The chromosomes become compact again,
and the nuclear membrane disappears.

37
00:01:40,691 --> 00:01:41,591
Stage two.

38
00:01:41,414 --> 00:01:42,314
Metaphase two.

39
00:01:42,302 --> 00:01:44,478
The chromosomes line up at the equator.

40
00:01:44,478 --> 00:01:48,564
Fibres from opposite poles attach to the
kinetochores of the sister chromatids.

41
00:01:49,314 --> 00:01:50,214
Stage three.

42
00:01:50,211 --> 00:01:51,153
Anaphase two.

43
00:01:51,153 --> 00:01:55,229
The centromere of each chromosome
splits, and the sister chromatids move

44
00:01:55,229 --> 00:01:56,458
to opposite poles.

45
00:01:57,208 --> 00:01:58,132
Stage four.

46
00:01:58,132 --> 00:01:59,207
Telophase two.

47
00:01:59,207 --> 00:02:03,274
A nuclear envelope forms around
each group of chromosomes again.

48
00:02:04,024 --> 00:02:04,924
Step five.

49
00:02:04,831 --> 00:02:06,249
The cytoplasm divides.

50
00:02:06,249 --> 00:02:10,541
We get a tetrad of cells: four haploid
daughter cells, from one starting

51
00:02:10,541 --> 00:02:11,441
cell.

52
00:02:11,810 --> 00:02:13,584
So why does meiosis matter?

53
00:02:13,584 --> 00:02:14,733
Two big reasons.

54
00:02:15,083 --> 00:02:15,983
One.

55
00:02:15,570 --> 00:02:19,776
It keeps the chromosome number the
same, generation after generation.

56
00:02:19,776 --> 00:02:23,102
Gametes get half, and
fertilisation joins two halves.

57
00:02:23,602 --> 00:02:27,078
In humans, each gamete has
twenty three chromosomes.

58
00:02:27,078 --> 00:02:30,940
Sperm plus egg gives forty
six, the same as each parent.

59
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:32,340
Two.

60
00:02:31,916 --> 00:02:33,204
It creates variety.

61
00:02:33,204 --> 00:02:37,237
Crossing over in pachytene mixes
pieces of the chromosomes from the two

62
00:02:37,237 --> 00:02:38,137
parents.

63
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:42,773
And in anaphase one, the pairs separate
in random ways, so every gamete

64
00:02:42,773 --> 00:02:43,956
gets a new mix.

65
00:02:43,956 --> 00:02:46,744
This variety is very
important for evolution.

66
00:02:46,744 --> 00:02:50,678
It is also why two children of
the same parents look different.

67
00:02:51,328 --> 00:02:54,318
Side by side, meiosis one and meiosis two.

68
00:02:54,318 --> 00:02:55,461
What separates.

69
00:02:55,461 --> 00:02:58,115
In meiosis one, homologous chromosomes.

70
00:02:58,115 --> 00:03:00,513
In meiosis two, sister chromatids.

71
00:03:01,263 --> 00:03:02,568
Chromosome number.

72
00:03:02,568 --> 00:03:05,484
Meiosis one halves it, from two n to n.

73
00:03:05,484 --> 00:03:07,918
Meiosis two keeps it, n stays n.

74
00:03:08,668 --> 00:03:09,568
Prophase.

75
00:03:09,379 --> 00:03:13,075
Prophase one is long, with five
substages and crossing over.

76
00:03:13,075 --> 00:03:15,190
Prophase two is short and simple.

77
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:16,840
Your turn.

78
00:03:16,725 --> 00:03:20,714
In a haploid cell, the centromeres
split and the sister chromatids move

79
00:03:20,714 --> 00:03:21,614
apart.

80
00:03:21,265 --> 00:03:23,115
Meiosis one, or meiosis two?

81
00:03:23,115 --> 00:03:24,270
Pause and decide.

82
00:03:27,470 --> 00:03:28,413
Meiosis two!

83
00:03:28,413 --> 00:03:32,908
In meiosis, the centromeres split only
in anaphase two, never in anaphase

84
00:03:32,908 --> 00:03:33,808
one.

85
00:03:34,054 --> 00:03:35,128
Remember this.

86
00:03:35,128 --> 00:03:37,185
Meiosis one halves the number.

87
00:03:37,185 --> 00:03:39,443
Meiosis two separates the sisters.

88
00:03:40,043 --> 00:03:41,683
Now, a worked example.

89
00:03:41,683 --> 00:03:42,583
Six events.

90
00:03:42,577 --> 00:03:44,567
Meiosis one, or meiosis two?

91
00:03:44,917 --> 00:03:47,234
Homologous chromosomes pair up.

92
00:03:47,234 --> 00:03:48,335
Meiosis one.

93
00:03:48,785 --> 00:03:49,893
The centromeres split.

94
00:03:49,893 --> 00:03:50,793
Meiosis two.

95
00:03:51,046 --> 00:03:52,167
Crossing over.

96
00:03:52,167 --> 00:03:53,184
Meiosis one.

97
00:03:53,634 --> 00:03:56,282
Sister chromatids move to opposite poles.

98
00:03:56,282 --> 00:03:57,248
Meiosis two.

99
00:03:57,698 --> 00:03:59,727
Bivalents line up at the equator.

100
00:03:59,727 --> 00:04:00,627
Meiosis one.

101
00:04:01,048 --> 00:04:03,283
And four haploid cells form.

102
00:04:03,283 --> 00:04:04,395
Meiosis two.

103
00:04:05,045 --> 00:04:06,507
Now, the exam trap.

104
00:04:06,857 --> 00:04:10,952
Many students think the DNA is copied
again before meiosis two, just like

105
00:04:10,952 --> 00:04:12,275
before every mitosis.

106
00:04:12,675 --> 00:04:15,198
But there is no DNA
copying in interkinesis.

107
00:04:15,198 --> 00:04:18,031
The DNA is copied only
once, before meiosis one.

108
00:04:18,031 --> 00:04:21,449
That is how the four cells end
up with half the chromosomes.

109
00:04:22,099 --> 00:04:23,509
Where is this asked?

110
00:04:23,509 --> 00:04:28,067
As the differences between meiosis one
and two, interkinesis, and why meiosis

111
00:04:28,067 --> 00:04:29,032
is important.

112
00:04:29,632 --> 00:04:30,809
Exam level now.

113
00:04:30,809 --> 00:04:32,135
Read the question.

114
00:04:32,135 --> 00:04:33,362
Pause and solve.

115
00:04:38,762 --> 00:04:40,172
The answer is B.

116
00:04:40,672 --> 00:04:44,869
In anaphase two, each centromere splits,
and the sister chromatids separate.

117
00:04:45,369 --> 00:04:48,989
Homologues separating and halving the
number happen in anaphase one, and

118
00:04:48,989 --> 00:04:50,951
terminalisation happens in diakinesis.

119
00:04:51,551 --> 00:04:53,092
Here is your recap.

120
00:04:53,092 --> 00:04:54,676
Screenshot this card.

121
00:04:57,876 --> 00:05:00,061
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

122
00:05:00,462 --> 00:05:01,362
One.

123
00:05:00,931 --> 00:05:02,863
What separates in anaphase two?

124
00:05:03,763 --> 00:05:04,663
Two.

125
00:05:04,153 --> 00:05:06,073
Is the DNA copied during interkinesis?

126
00:05:06,973 --> 00:05:07,873
Three.

127
00:05:07,470 --> 00:05:09,729
Give two reasons why meiosis is important.

128
00:05:10,829 --> 00:05:14,598
Next time: how does a green
leaf turn sunlight into food?

129
00:05:14,598 --> 00:05:17,605
We find out in photosynthesis
in higher plants.
